course: dictionary
lesson: 7057
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words wear more than one coat. The word pasty can be a food, a way of describing a face, or a thick, sticky mixture. Which meaning is right depends on the words around it, so read the whole sentence before you decide.
explain: «The ancient warders were dozing over a pasty.» Here a pasty is something you eat. Warders are guards, and a guard on a lunch break would have a pastry filled with meat and vegetables. Ask yourself: does my meaning fit the sentence?
explain: «She had a pasty face after weeks indoors.» Now pasty describes a face. The clue is «after weeks indoors»: she has not been outside, so her face looks pale and unhealthy, like dough. A «pasty-faced» child is one who looks ill, tired or pale.
explain: In «The mixture was pasty and thick, like paste», pasty means sticky. Each time, the words around the word told you its meaning.
fact: Pasty can be a noun (a pasty, pasties, said PAST-ee) or an adjective (pasty, pastier, pastiest, said PAYST-ee).
fact: «A pasty» means a pastry case folded around a filling of meat and vegetables, then baked. The Cornish pasty is famous.
fact: In UK and Cornish life, pasties are a lunch food, and Cornish miners once took them underground, holding the thick crust edge.
fact: «Pasty» as an adjective can describe a face or skin that is pale and unhealthy-looking, like dough.
fact: «Pasty» can also mean thick and sticky, like paste.
exercise: «The ancient warders were dozing over a pasty.» What is a pasty in this sentence? | A thick, sticky glue | A pale, unhealthy face | A tall stone tower | *A pastry case with a meat and vegetable filling
exercise: «He had a pasty face after weeks in bed.» What does pasty describe? | A sticky mess | A baked lunch | *A pale, unhealthy-looking face | A bright, sunny face
exercise: Sam says «pasty» is only a food. What does pasty mean in «pale and pasty»? | *Pale and unhealthy-looking | Warm from the oven | Shiny and wet | Full of meat and vegetables
test: In «She looked pasty after weeks indoors», what does pasty mean? | A baked meat pastry | Thick and sticky | *Pale and unhealthy-looking | Bright and rosy
test: In «The glue was pasty, thick and hard to spread», what does pasty mean? | Crisp and golden | Pale and ill | Cold to touch | *Thick and sticky, like paste
exam: In «The children ate a Cornish pasty on the beach», what does pasty mean? | Pale and unhealthy | Sleepy and tired | *A pastry with a meat and vegetable filling | Thick and sticky
